/></dqv:QualityAnnotation></dqv:hasQualityAnnotation></ore:Aggregation><ore:Proxy rdf:about="http://data.europeana.eu/proxy/europeana/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"><dc:format rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/concept/2090"></dc:format><dc:identifier>http://mint-projects.image.ntua.gr/europeana-fashion/RP-P-2005-96</dc:identifier><dc:language>nld</dc:language><dcterms:spatial rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/place/85"></dcterms:spatial><edm:europeanaProxy>true</edm:europeanaProxy><ore:proxyFor rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/item/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"/><ore:proxyIn rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/aggregation/europeana/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"/><ore:lineage rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/proxy/provider/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"/><edm:type>IMAGE</edm:type></ore:Proxy><ore:Proxy rdf:about="http://data.europeana.eu/proxy/provider/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"><dc:description xml:lang="en">In the 1680s and 90s, Paris engravers produced a large number of fashion prints that combined illustrations with short texts about clothing or the wearer. They developed various strategies to promote these ‘fashions’. In the print after Dieu de Saint-Jean, an anonymous woman departs for the city wearing a new style of over-garment called a manteau. By contrast, Trouvain and Bonnart relied on the power of celebrity by featuring prominent aristocrats dressed à la mode. These are not true portraits, though; the printmakers merely paired famous names with anonymous figures.</dc:description><dc:description xml:lang="nl">Parijse graveurs produceerden in de jaren 80 en 90 grote aantallen modeprenten in combinatie met een korte tekst over de kleding of de draagster ervan. Zij gebruikten verschillende strategieën om de Franse modes in de markt te zetten. Op de prent naar Dieu de Saint-Jean toont een anonieme dame een nieuw type overkleed, de manteau, als zij naar de stad gaat. Trouvain en Bonnart daarentegen gebruikten beroemdheden in hun prenten: prominente aristocraten in een modieuze outfit. Het zijn echter geen echte portretten; de graveurs koppelden bekende namen aan anonieme figuren.</dc:description><dc:format xml:lang="en">paper</dc:format><dc:format xml:lang="nl">papier</dc:format><dc:identifier>RP-P-2005-96</dc:identifier><dc:language>nl</dc:language><dc:subject xml:lang="nl">1675 - 1699</dc:subject><dc:title xml:lang="en">French Prints Propagating French Fashion : Portrait of Catherine de Neuville, Countess of Armagnac</dc:title><dc:title xml:lang="nl">Franse prenten propageren Franse mode : Portret van Catherine de Neuville, gravin van Armagnac</dc:title><dc:type rdf:resource="http://thesaurus.europeanafashion.eu/thesaurus/10000"></dc:type><dc:type xml:lang="en">Object Type: fashion objects</dc:type><dcterms:created xml:lang="en">c.1685 - c.1695</dcterms:created><dcterms:created xml:lang="nl">ca.1685 - ca.1695</dcterms:created><dcterms:extent xml:lang="en">height 275 mm</dcterms:extent><dcterms:extent xml:lang="en">width 188 mm</dcterms:extent><dcterms:extent xml:lang="nl">hoogte 275 mm</dcterms:extent><dcterms:extent xml:lang="nl">breedte 188 mm</dcterms:extent><dcterms:spatial rdf:resource="https://sws.geonames.org/3017382/"></dcterms:spatial><edm:europeanaProxy>false</edm:europeanaProxy><ore:proxyFor rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/item/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"/><ore:proxyIn rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/aggregation/provider/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"/><edm:type>IMAGE</edm:type></ore:Proxy><edm:EuropeanaAggregation rdf:about="http://data.europeana.eu/aggregation/europeana/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"><edm:aggregatedCHO rdf:resource="http://data.europeana.eu/item/739/europeana_fashion_RP_P_2005_96"/><edm:datasetName>739_EuropeanaFashion_1121</edm:datasetName><edm:country>Netherlands</edm:country><edm:preview rdf:resource="https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/bldR5n4vln9UBPuL7uj0xCQj5NT2SVcq-L2cmtqYrcOIgnUeSgDYagVDRtJqUbpv36V1